Continual-ARC demos can vary radically in grid size, palette, and apparent motif. Do not conclude they are unrelated solely from those differences, and do not unify them based on superficial shared colors or density. For each pair, extract the exact input-output changes and describe the transformation in role-level terms (background, marker/seed, copied footprint, output color). Compare those abstractions across all pairs. A compelling candidate must reproduce every demo cell-for-cell, including cells left unchanged and explicit background-valued positions in any stamped footprint. If no common validated rule emerges, another demo is more valuable than a speculative submission when the remaining budget permits.

A useful audit table records per demo: dimensions; color roles; seed/motif offsets; changed cells; proposed abstract operation; exact replay result. Distinguish a genuinely conditional rule from an incorrect attempt to force one unconditional template. Keep the final action protocol exact regardless of analysis length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-role-abstraction.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demonstrations: reconcile before transferring

When the same instance supplies demonstrations with widely different dimensions, background colors, seed colors, and motif shapes, do not assume the visible seed in the test is enough to derive a familiar operation. First record each pair's input/output changed-cell mask, foreground/background roles, and unchanged seed/background cells. Search for one role-level invariant that explains all pairs; explicitly reject candidate rules such as zero-component flood fill or global recoloring unless they reproduce every output exactly.

After each failed submission, revisit the transformation hypothesis rather than changing arbitrary thresholds or component definitions. If the demo cap is exhausted, use remaining submissions only for a rule supported by the full evidence. This instance's guesses of connected-background recoloring were not demonstrated by cross-pair replay and failed; the reusable lesson is to validate cross-demo invariance before committing, not to favor another particular rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md", "content": "# Selecting demos for heterogeneous ARC examples

When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md", "content": "# Attempt-cap handling and retry gate

In strict one-action puzzle interfaces, the protocol remains binding after the puzzle ends. When feedback says the instance is over—especially after exhausting the attempt cap—respond with exactly `{"action":"finish"}`. An empty assistant response or another `submit` is not a valid terminal action.

After a wrong submission, binary feedback gives no location information. Do not respond with a cosmetic or arbitrary variant (for example, changing only a suspected region's color). State the proposed model correction internally and test it against the complete demonstrations, including unchanged pairs and preserved cells. Only submit when the revised rule has evidence and reproduces all examples. If ambiguity remains and a demo is available, request one only to resolve a specific competing hypothesis; once demos are exhausted, further unsupported guesses compound cost and risk consuming the cap.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md"}

